# Untitled

Microwave-assisted synthesis of ultrafine Au nanoparticles immobilized on MOF-199 in high loading as efficient catalysts for a three-component coupling reaction
Yan Jiang\(^{1}\), Xin Zhang\(^{1}\) (✉), Xiaoping Dai\(^{1}\), Wen Zhang\(^{1}\), Qiang Sheng\(^{1}\), Hongying Zhu\(^{1}\), Yun Xiao\(^{1}\), and Hai Wang\(^{2}\)
\(^{1}\)State Key Laboratory of Heavy Oil Processing, China University of Petroleum, Beijing 102249, China \(^{2}\)National Institute of Metrology, Beijing 100013, China
© Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2016
## KEYWORDS
MOF-199, ultrafine Au nanoparticles (NPs), high loading, microwave irradiation, A\(^{3}\)-coupling
## ABSTRACT
Controlled integration of ultrafine metal nanoparticles (MNPs) and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) has drawn much attention due to their unique physical and chemical properties. However, the development of a one-step strategy for preparing ultrafine MNPs within MOFs still remains a great challenge. Herein, a facile synthetic approach toward the abovementioned composites was developed. In contrast to the conventional approach, these hybrids were prepared by the direct mixing of metal and MOF precursors in the reaction solution assisted by microwave irradiation. Impressively, the Au/MOF-199 composite with uniformly distributed ultrafine Au nanoparticles could be fabricated in only two minutes, and the Au loading could be increased up to a level of \(5.13\%\). The multifunctional Au/MOF-199 catalysts exhibited high turnover numbers (TONs) and turnover frequencies (TOFs) in the three-component coupling reaction of formaldehyde, phenylacetylene, and piperidine (A\(^{3}\)-coupling). Owing to the confinement effect of MOF-199, the \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) catalyst could be recycled for five runs without serious loss of activity, with no obvious aggregation of Au NPs detected.
## 1 Introduction
Metal nanoparticles (MNPs) have attracted much attention because of their unique physicochemical properties compared to bulk counterparts [1] and a
wide range of potential applications in energy conversion, environmental remediation, drug research, sensing [2], and catalysis [3]. The preparation of MNPs with diameters smaller than \(2\,\mathrm{nm}\) is critical and scientifically important due to their dramatically
improved catalytic performance [4], mainly owing to the high degree of dispersion and a remarkable nanosize effect. For instance, the epoxidation of propylene catalyzed by Au nanoparticles (NPs) larger than \(2\,\mathrm{nm}\) mainly produces epoxypropane, while Au NPs smaller than \(2\,\mathrm{nm}\) primarily yield propane [5, 6]. However, controlling the ultrafine size \((<2\,\mathrm{nm})\) of MNPs is difficult, especially for Au NPs, due to their high surface energies and low melting points [7]. Therefore, much effort has been devoted to protecting MNP clusters from aggregation, e.g., by introducing capping agents [8, 9] and distributing MNPs/clusters on supports [10].
Currently, most synthetic methods deal with the preparation of Au-based catalysts [11]. For instance, the co-precipitation and the deposition-precipitation method of Zhang et al. [12, 13] are widely used. These catalysts are generally supported on metal oxides and graphene. For example, Qiao et al. prepared a composite of single-atom gold dispersed on \(\mathrm{FeO}_{x}\) \((0.03\,\mathrm{wt.}\%)\) [14], which exhibited extremely high stability in the CO oxidation reaction. Li et al. reported an efficient catalytic system featuring Au NPs homogeneously distributed on \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) surface with an ultra-low loading of \(0.09\,\mathrm{wt.}\%\) [15], while Banhart synthesized individual Au atoms supported on graphene [16]. Thus, it can be seen that the low loading of single Au atoms or its clusters immobilized on supports has already been realized. However, the encapsulation of tiny Au clusters (diameter \(<2\,\mathrm{nm}\)) with high loading \((>5\%)\) is highly anticipated and challenging. Nanoporous materials may be promising candidates for enhancing the metal loading because of their unique pore structure [17].
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are assembled using principles of coordination chemistry, featuring metal ions or clusters bridged by organic linkers [18, 19], not only possessing a large surface area together with a tunable pore size and volume, but also exhibiting other exciting characteristics including a well-defined structure, multiple coordination sites, and exceptional thermal and chemical stability. Due to these properties, the use of MOFs has attracted much attention in applications such as gas sorption [20, 21], separation [22], catalysis [23], drug delivery [24], optics [25], and sensing [2]. Recently, based on the confinement effect of their crystalline pore structure,
MOFs have been elegantly employed as supports for developing solid catalysts with fine monometal or alloy particles [26–28].
Conventionally, two-step methods such as chemical vapor deposition [29, 30], solid grinding [31–33], and solution infiltration [34–36] are used to prepare MOF-supported metal clusters. Initially, MOFs were synthesized over several or dozens of hours, soaked in the metal precursor solution, and reduced (typically with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}\)) to obtain MNPs deposited on MOFs. However, the inherent limitations of this strategy include the damage of MOF structure during postreduction processing and poor control over the MNP size. Moreover, two-step methods are too cumbersome and time-consuming. Earlier, our group has reported a one-pot synthesis of Au NPs supported on IRMOF-3 that takes about \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) [37]. In order to simplify the preparation process and improve the synthesis efficiency, a one-pot method requiring a small amount of time is highly desirable. Most researchers have focused on the microwave irradiation method because of its unique advantages, such as efficiency, fast crystallization, and narrow particle-size distribution. This method has already been reported for preparing Pd and Pt NPs [38], Au–Rh core–shell NPs [39], MOF-5 [40, 41], Ag-doped MOFs [42], Ln-MOFs, etc. [43]. However, the fabrication of ultrafine Au NPs immobilized on MOFs under microwave irradiation has not been reported so far.
In this study, MOF-199 \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{TMA})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right]_{3}\right)\), \(\mathrm{TMA}=1,3,5\)-trimesic acid) was selected as a support. Impressively, \(\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) hybrids with ultrafine Au NPs were successfully synthesized by a facile one-pot microwave irradiation method in only two minutes, and the Au loading could be increased up to \(5.13\%\). Herein, Au and MOF precursors \(\left(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{2})_{2}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BTC}\) were directly mixed into the reaction solution containing \(N,N\)-dimethylformamide (DMF), ethanol, and polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), which was subsequently heated by microwave irradiation (Scheme 1). As multifunctional catalysts, the hybrids exhibited high turnover numbers (TONs) and turnover frequencies (TOFs) in the three-component coupling reaction \(\left(\mathrm{A}^{3}\right.\)-coupling). Owing to the confinement effect of MOF-199, \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) could be recycled for five runs
without a serious loss of activity and significant aggregation of Au NPs.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Tetrachloroauric acid \(\mathrm{(HAuCl_{4}{\cdot}3H_{2}O)}\), silver nitrate \(\mathrm{(AgNO_{3})}\), chloroplatinic acid \(\mathrm{(H_{2}PtCl_{6}{\cdot}6H_{2}O)}\), polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP; \(M_{\mathrm{W}}=30{,}000\)), copper nitrate trihydrate \(\mathrm{(Cu(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3H_{2}O)}\), and 1,3,5-trimesic acid \(\mathrm{(H_{3}BTC)}\) were of analytical grade and were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Chloroform \(\left(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\right)\), ethanol, and DMF were of analytical grade and were purchased from the Beijing Chemical Reagent Company. All chemicals were sourced from commercial suppliers and used without further purification.
## 2.2 Synthesis of pure MOF-199
\(\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3H_{2}O}\) \((54.4\;\mathrm{mg})\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) \((48.2\;\mathrm{mg})\) were dissolved in DMF \((10\,\mathrm{mL})\). The solution was transferred into a \(60{\-}\mathrm{mL}\) microwave autoclave and stirred for \(10\,\mathrm{{min}}\) at room temperature. Subsequently, PVP \((702.5\;\mathrm{mg})\) was added to the above solution, followed by further stirring for \(60\,\mathrm{{min}}\). After complete PVP dissolution, the autoclave was placed into a microwave oven (microwave reaction instrument: MD6C-6H 213088), heated to \(120\,^{\circ}C\) at a rate of \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\), and held for \(2\,\mathrm{min}\). The green-colored reaction mixture was centrifuged at \(9{,}900\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\), furnishing the
blue product as a precipitate, which was several times washed with ethanol. Subsequently, the solid was soaked in \(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and centrifuged to remove DMF. Finally, the product was dried using a vacuum rotary evaporator at \(65\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\).
## 2.3 One-pot synthesis of Au/MOF-199 with ultrafine Au NPs
The synthetic conditions and procedures were similar to that used for pure MOF-199. Typically, \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((54.4\;\mathrm{mg})\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) \((48.2\;\mathrm{mg})\) were dissolved in DMF \((10\,\mathrm{mL})\) in a \(60{\-}\mathrm{mL}\) microwave autoclave, and the solution was stirred for \(10\,\mathrm{{min}}\) at room temperature. Subsequently, PVP \((1,000.0\;\mathrm{mg})\) was added, followed by 60-min stirring. After PVP dissolution, the pre-fabricated \(\mathrm{HAuCl_{4}{\cdot}3H_{2}O}\) solution \((20\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\,(20\,\mathrm{mM});\) 30, 60, 100, or \(120\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) \((100\,\mathrm{mM})\) was dropwise added into the above reaction mixture and further stirred for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\), followed by transfer to the microwave oven. The oven was heated to \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) at a rate of \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) and held for \(2\,\mathrm{min}\). After microwave treatment, the solution was centrifuged at \(9{,}900\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\). The obtained blue product was three times washed with ethanol. Subsequently, the solid was soaked in \(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and centrifuged to remove DMF. Finally, the product was dried using a vacuum rotary evaporator at \(65\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\). The synthetic procedures for Pt/MOF-199 and \(\mathrm{Ag/MOF-199}\) are the same as that for \(\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\), with \(\mathrm{HAuCl_{4}{\cdot}3H_{2}O}\) replaced by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\) \((80\,\mathrm{mM})\) or \(\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\) \((102\;\mathrm{mM})\) aqueous solutions.
## 2.4 Hydrothermal synthesis of Au/MOF-199 nanostructures
\(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((5.4\;\mathrm{mg})\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) \((4.8\;\mathrm{mg})\) were dissolved in DMF \((8\,\mathrm{mL})\), and the solution was stirred for \(10\,\mathrm{{min}}\). Subsequently, PVP \((1,000.0\,\mathrm{mg})\) was added to the above solution and further stirred for \(60\,\mathrm{{min}}\) at room temperature. After complete dissolution, the pre-fabricated \(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution \((10\,\mathrm{mM};\,10,\,20,\,30,\) or \(40\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) was dropwise added into the above reaction mixture and further stirred for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\). The resulting homogeneous solution was transferred into a \(25{\-}\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave. The vessel was sealed, placed into a drying oven, and heated at \(140\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) before being cooled down to room temperature. The obtained product was centrifuged, washed with ethanol for three times, soaked in \(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\), and centrifuged again to remove DMF. Finally, the product was dried using a vacuum rotary evaporator at \(65\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(30\,\mathrm{{min}}\) for further characterization.
## 2.5 Catalyst characterization
The size and morphology of the produced nanostructures were determined by transmission electron microscopy (TEM; JEM 2100, \(200\;\mathrm{kV}\)) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM; Tecnai G2 F20 S-Twin, \(200\,\mathrm{kV}\)). TEM samples were prepared by drop-casting ethanolic dispersions onto carbon-coated copper grids with a pipettor and allowing the solvent to evaporate. High-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) was conducted on a Tecnai G2 F20 S-Twin HRTEM instrument operating at \(200\,\mathrm{kV}\). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) imaging was performed using an ULTRA 55 scanning electron microscope at \(200\,\mathrm{K}\). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on a Bruker D8-advance diffractometer operated at \(40\,\mathrm{kV}\) and \(40\,\mathrm{mA}\) using \(\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda\,=\,1.5406\,\mathrm{\AA})\). Inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) measurements were performed on an IRIS Intrepid II XSP instrument (Thermo Fisher), with the following working parameters: RF power, \(1,150\;\mathrm{W}\); nebulizer flow, \(26.0\,\mathrm{psi}\); auxiliary gas, \(1.0\ \mathrm{LPM}\). Surface areas and pore size distributions were characterized with the help of nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms recorded
on a Micromeritics ASAP 2010 system at \(-196\,^{\circ}\!C\). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was performed on a PHI 5000 Versaprobe system using monochromatic Al Kα radiation \((1,486.6\,\mathrm{eV})\).
## 2.6 Catalytic reactions
The catalytic performance of pure MOF-199 and Au/ MOF-199 catalysts was evaluated in the \(\mathrm{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction. Paraformaldehyde (0.25 or \(0.5\;\mathrm{mmol}\)) was dispersed in 1,4-dioxane \((1,000.0\;\mathrm{mg})\) and stirred for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\). After complete dissolution, the catalysts (50 or \(5\,\mathrm{mg}\)) were added, and the mixture was ultrasonicated for \(2\,\mathrm{min}\). Subsequently, certain amounts of phenylacetylene and piperidine were added into the above solution. During this process, the molar ratio of formaldehyde/phenylacetylene/piperidine was kept constant at \(1/1.3/1.2\). Next, the obtained solution was transferred into a microwave autoclave and heated with microwave radiation at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\). The obtained organic solvent was diluted with 1,4-dioxane and analyzed by gas chromatography (SP-6890). The catalysts were separated by centrifugation and three times washed with 1,4-dioxane. To estimate Au leaching during the reaction, a hot filtration experiment was conducted to determine whether the reaction proceeded in a heterogeneous or homogeneous fashion [44, 45]. After a 10-min reaction time, the catalyst was separated by hot filtration, and the filtrate was further allowed to react under the same conditions for another \(50\,\mathrm{{min}}\). The liquid phase was analyzed by ICP-OES.
## 2.7 Recycling of the \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) catalyst
For recyclability tests, the reactions were performed under the same conditions as described above, except for using the recovered catalyst. Each time after the catalytic reaction, the catalyst was separated from the reaction mixture by centrifugation, thoroughly washed with 1,4-dioxane, and dried at \(80\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(10\,\mathrm{{h}}\).
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of Au/MOF-199 with different Au loadings
In this study, the morphology and structure of Au/ MOF-199 hybrids prepared under controlled reaction
To demonstrate the generality of this facile microwave-assisted synthetic strategy, other MNPs (such as Ag and Pt) have been explored as well. By carefully controlling the metal precursor amount, ultrafine Ag/MOF-199 and Pt/MOF-199 were prepared by the one-pot microwave-assisted method (Fig. S2 in the ESM). We believe that our method provides a simple and convenient route to building blocks for
MNP/MOF nanostructures.
The SEM and TEM images of \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) (Figs. 2(a) and 2(b), respectively) show that it consists of octahedral particles \((>\!99\%)\). HAADF-STEM and energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) elemental mapping were used to further probe the above structure. As shown in Fig. 2(c), the highly dispersed bright spots are uniformly distributed, while elemental mapping demonstrates the even distribution of Au, Cu, C, and O throughout the entire structure of MOF-199 (Figs. 2(d)–2(g)).
The structures of pure MOF-199 and Au/MOF-199 synthesized by the microwave irradiation method were characterized by XRD (Fig. 3(a)). The diffraction peaks of pure MOF-199 agreed well with the ones reported previously [36], indicating the successful synthesis of MOF-199. Meanwhile, the peaks of Au/ MOF-199 were also well matched with those of regular octahedral MOF-199. It should be noted that no identifiable peaks assignable to Au NPs were detected for the hybrids. This observation may due to the low Au concentration and small particle size, further demonstrating the highly dispersed nature of Au NPs.
The nanostructures were additionally probed by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption. Figure 3(b) presents the
corresponding typical type I isotherms, characteristic of a microporous structure. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of as-synthesized pure MOF-199, \(0.22\%\) , \(1.82\%\) , \(3.25\%\) , and \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) were 852, 843, 812, 768, and \(719\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},\) respectively. Apparently, the BET surface areas decreased with increasing Au content. Compared to pure MOF-199, the average pore diameter of Au/MOF-199 decreased from 1.43 to \(1.41\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Table S1 in the ESM), which could be attributed to some of the MOF-199 pores being blocked by the loaded Au NPs.
To gain further insight into the catalyst structure, in particular the location of Au and \(\mathrm{Cu}\), these materials were characterized by XPS. Figure 4(a) shows the Au 4f XPS spectra of Au/MOF-199. It can be seen that the peak intensity increased with increasing Au loading, suggesting that Au is present mainly on the external surface. The samples featured very close \(4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) and \(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\) binding energies of 84.0 and \(87.8\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively. Based on earlier reports, Au \(4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) binding energies of
83.6–84.5 eV are characteristic of \(\mathrm{Au^{0}}\) [46]. Therefore, all Au/MOF-199 composites feature metallic gold species. In addition, Fig. 4(b) shows the corresponding Cu \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) XPS spectra. The obvious "shake-up" peaks indicate the existence of \(C\mathbf{u}^{2+}\). The peak at \(933.7\,\mathrm{eV}\) is attributed to \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\), while the one at \(931.9\,\mathrm{eV}\) is characteristic of \({\mathrm{Cu(I)}}\). All binding energies were in good agreement with the manual.
The detailed formation mechanism for the \(5.13\%\) Au/MOF-199 nanostructure was elucidated using a series of controllable parameters, including the amount of added PVP, ethanol/DMF ratio, and heating form. PVP, which is added to the reaction solution during synthesis, does not only act as a stabilizer to promote the dispersion of Au NPs, but also provides the affinity to adsorb MOF-199 precursors and Au NPs via hydrophobic interactions between the apolar groups of PVP and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) organic linkers, also furnishing the coordination interaction between pyrrolidone rings \((\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O})\) and \(C\mathbf{u}^{2+}\) [47]. To further understand the coordination interaction, ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) absorption spectroscopy analyses were carried out. The UV–vis absorption spectra of \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) and PVP solutions showed absorption peaks at 221 and \(224\,\mathrm{nm}\), respectively (Fig. 5(a)). When the above solutions were mixed, a peak at \(299\,\mathrm{nm}\) was observed, indicating interaction between these components. In addition, absorption peaks at 240 and \(282\,\mathrm{nm}\) were detected for \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) (Fig. 5(b)), and the appearance of a peak at \(293\,\mathrm{nm}\) indicated coordination between PVP and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\). It can be thus assumed that the MOF should grow on the Au surface as opposed to self-nucleation. Figure S3 (in the ESM) shows that the average size of Au NPs can be controlled between 5.3 and \(1.6\,\mathrm{nm}\) by changing the PVP amount from 260 to \(1{,}300\;\mathrm{mg}\), respectively, illustrating that the size of Au NPs decreases with increasing amount of added PVP.
The solvent used in the reaction was pivotal for the preparation of Au/MOF-199 with ultrafine Au NPs. The above nanostructure was successfully prepared in pure DMF (Fig. 1(d)). The preparation of MOF-199 was reported to be accompanied by the formation of well-dispersed \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\) NPs [36].
However, it was difficult to distinguish Au NPs from \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\) NPs because of their similar sizes. Nevertheless, a slight aggregation of particles could be observed
when \(1\;\mathrm{mL}\) of ethanol was added to the solution (Fig. 6(a)). Moreover, as the ethanol/DMF volume ratio changed from \(1/9\) to \(2/8\) and \(3/7\), the particles appeared to be seriously aggregated (Figs. 6(b) and 6(c)). The spacing between two adjacent lattice fringes determined by HRTEM imaging of the agglomerated darker particles was about \(0.235\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 6(d)). This value is in good agreement with the spacing of (111) planes of Au, suggesting that the agglomerated particles were Au NPs rather than \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\) NPs. DMF is commonly used as a solvent in the synthesis of various MOFs [48], also being able to act as a mild reducing agent at high temperatures [49]. Based on the well-established hard and soft acids and bases (HSAB) principle [50, 51], the generated Au NPs should preferentially coordinate to the soft \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N}\) group of DMF, while the hard oxophilic \(C\mathbf{u}^{2+}\) cations of the MOF should preferentially interact with the hard \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}\) group of DMF, providing a bridge between MOF precursors and
Au NPs. To further reveal the function of DMF, its interaction with \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) was investigated by UV–vis spectroscopy (Fig. 7). The peaks observed at 221 and \(238\,\mathrm{nm}\) were assigned to \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and DMF, respectively. When the \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) solution was added to DMF, a peak at \(300\,\mathrm{nm}\) appeared, suggesting an interaction between the above components. As is known, Au cations are easily reduced under the highly energetic microwave irradiation, with the rate of MOF generation in pure DMF solution also being fast. It can be deduced that the use of PVP resulted in a fraction of Au NPs being captured within the MOF, as mentioned above.
However, a part of Au cations were not reduced, still being present in solution. Therefore, the above reduced Au NPs were highly dispersed on the MOF external surface due to the tractive effect of DMF rather than self-growth. Meanwhile, the formed MOF could serve as a protective layer to protect the tiny Au clusters from aggregation during synthesis. On the contrary, no strong interaction existed between ethanol and Au or \(\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}\), resulting in the self-growth of Au NPs and their aggregation into larger clusters. Based on the above results, it can be concluded that ethanol promotes Au aggregation in this procedure.
Finally, it is reasonable to infer that the rapidly increasing reactant temperature under microwave irradiation was another critical factor for preparing \(\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) with ultrafine gold NPs. To the best of our knowledge, the traditional hydrothermal technique and the microwave irradiation method exhibit different heating mechanisms. The conventional heating method is rather slow and inefficient for transferring energy to the reaction solution, since it mainly relies on convective currents and the thermal conductivity of materials [52]. Consequently, the temperature of the reaction vessel is higher than that of the reaction solution. Usually, crystals nucleate easily near the walls or on dust particles, which results in a small amount of seeds in solution. Therefore, the abundant residual reactants will sustain growth to larger NPs [52]. In contrast, microwave irradiation can realize efficient internal "in-core" heating, since the temperature is raised uniformly throughout the whole liquid volume by the direct coupling of microwave energy to the material molecules [53]. Therefore, the reactants can quickly nucleate in solution. As is known, a higher concentration of nucleation sites results in a lower concentration of reactants left to feed such seeds, which produces relatively small-sized particles [54, 55]. The nucleation and growth processes are schematically illustrated in Fig. S4 in the ESM. Owing to the simultaneous nucleation of crystals under microwave irradiation conditions, it is easy to produce a narrow size distribution of particles, such as ultrafine Au NPs.
## 3.2 Catalytic performance in the \(\mathbf{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction
In recent years, with the increasing environmental consciousness of the chemical community, more
attention has been devoted to finding efficient and environmentally friendly chemical synthesis methods [56], a representative example being propargylation. As is known, propargylamines are versatile intermediates in organic synthesis, also being important structural elements of natural products and therapeutic drug molecules [57, 58]. In this work, the \(\mathrm{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction was chosen to evaluate the activity of the nanostructures. Figure 8 compares the formaldehyde conversions of pure MOF-199 and Au/MOF-199 catalysts at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) under microwave irradiation. For comparison, pure MOF-199, \(0.22\%\), and \(1.82\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) catalysts were evaluated under identical reaction conditions (Fig. 8(a)). Kinetic analysis manifested the higher activity of Au/MOF-199 compared to pure MOF-199. Formaldehyde conversions of \(15\%\) and \(45\%\) were obtained for gold loadings of \(0.22\%\) and \(1.82\%\), respectively, at a reaction time of \(5\,\mathrm{min}\), with a continuous increase up to \(86\%\) and \(93\%\) at \(30\,\mathrm{min}\). In contrast, a conversion of only \(7\%\) occurred
in the presence of pure MOF-199 after \(20\,\mathrm{min}\), slightly increasing to \(10\%\) after \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) under identical conditions. The propargylamine selectivity for pure MOF-199 was only \(21\%\), giving rise to a corresponding yield of ca. \(2\%\). Thus, the activity of pure MOF-199 could be neglected, meaning that the ultrafine Au NPs are highly active sites for the \(\mathrm{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction. The selectivity toward the desired propargylamine product was equal to \(100\%\) for all Au/MOF-199 catalysts. The TONs were calculated based on the number of exposed Au atoms at the surface, which was estimated from the mean Au NP diameter and their actual loading. In this case, \(0.22\%\) and \(1.82\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) showed TONs of 346 and 56 (at \(30\,\mathrm{min}\)), while the TOFs were calculated to be as high as 723 and \(323\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), respectively, at a reaction time of \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) (Table 1). It can be seen that the TON and TOF calculated for the high conversions of \(1.82\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) were seriously reduced, inferring that not all exposed Au atoms could be fully utilized under these conditions. To exclude the transport limitation, additional experiments with a lower amount of catalysts and a higher amount of reactants were conducted for \(1.82\%\), \(3.25\%\), and \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\). In this case, relatively lower formaldehyde conversions of \(5\%\), \(6\%\), and \(10\%\) were observed for \(1.82\%\), \(3.25\%\), and \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\), respectively (at \(5\,\mathrm{min}\)), continuously increasing to \(30\%\), \(40\%\), and \(59\%\) at \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) (Fig. 8(b)). For low formaldehyde conversions at 5 minutes, the influence of external diffusion on Au/MOF-199 with high loadings of gold could be ruled out. TOFs of 717, 713, and \(738\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) (at \(5\,\mathrm{min}\)) and TONs of 358, 365, and 363 (at \(30\,\mathrm{min}\)) were observed for \(1.82\%\), \(3.25\%\), and \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\), respectively. Analysis of the above results shows that the TONs and TOFs of Au/MOF-199 with high gold loadings were similar to those obtained for low loadings. This is remarkable, since these similar catalytic activities reveal that the exposed gold atoms of Au/MOF-199 were equally active in the \(\mathrm{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction, also demonstrating that these atoms are \(100\%\) accessible and fully used for the reaction between the low and high gold. It is evident that Au/MOF-199 with a higher gold loading of \(5.13\%\) possesses a much higher density of active sites. However, the previously reported heterogeneous supported gold catalysts, \(\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\), exhibit
Table 1 \(\mathrm{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction catalyzed by pure MOF-199 and Au/MOF-199 in 1,4-dioxane<sup>a</sup>
high catalytic activities in the \(\mathrm{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction, displaying TONs and TOFs as high as 788 and \(66\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), and 668 and \(56\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\), respectively [13]. Apparently, the TOF of \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) in this work is one order of magnitude higher than that of \(\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) catalysts. It has also been reported that Au/IRMOF-3 and unsupported Au nanoparticles are active catalysts for the \(\mathrm{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction [37, 59]. Besides, homogeneous gold salts [60], organic gold complexes [61], silver salts [62], and copper salts [63] can afford good aldehyde conversions, but are difficult to separate and reuse. Furthermore, the homogenous catalysts generally exhibited TONs not exceeding 100 (Table S2 in the ESM). XRD and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption experiments were used to probe the Au/MOF-199 catalyst after the reaction. As shown in Fig. S5(a) in the ESM, all diffraction peaks well matched the simulated peaks of pure MOF-199, suggesting that the samples retain their crystallinity. The BET surface areas of \(0.22\%\), \(1.82\%\), \(3.25\%\), and \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) were unchanged after the reaction (Fig. S5(b) in the ESM), equaling 840, 814, 765, and \(718\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), respectively, which confirmed the absence of collapse and preservation of the framework integrity. Moreover, a hot filtration experiment was performed to confirm the heterogeneity of the catalyst. After a reaction time of \(10\;\mathrm{min}\), the Au/MOF-199 catalyst was separated by hot filtration
and the filtrate was allowed to react under identical reaction conditions for another \(50\;\mathrm{min}\). As a result, no further conversion was detected after the catalyst removal (Fig. S6 in the ESM). ICP analysis results show that the content of free Au in the reaction mixture was very low (0.01, 0.01, 0.02, and \(0.03\;\mathrm{ppm}\), respectively, for \(0.22\%\), \(1.82\%\), \(3.25\%\), and \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\)). In the described procedure, PVP and DMF realize a strong Au–MOF interaction in the one-pot synthesis, which can inhibit the migration and aggregation of Au. The slight leaching of Au may be due to the insufficient strength of the above interaction. Finally, the catalytic performance stability of \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) was evaluated under the previously used conditions. As shown in Fig. S7(a) in the ESM, \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) could be recycled for five runs without a significant loss of performance, exhibiting stable activity for this reaction. XRD characterization of the recycled catalyst showed that its crystalline structure was retained, while the BET surface area decreased to \(701\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) (Figs. S7(b) and S7(c) in the ESM). In addition, the \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) catalyst was well characterized by TEM (Fig. S7(d) in the ESM). The diameter of ultrafine Au NPs increased slightly to \(1.7\;\mathrm{nm}\), and no serious aggregation could be observed. This result perfectly demonstrated that MOF-199 is well able to protect the NPs from aggregating, owing
to its confinement effect. Moreover, a hot filtration experiment was performed after five runs. After a reaction time of \(10\,\mathrm{min}\), the \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) catalyst was separated, and the filtrate was reacted under identical conditions for another \(50\;\mathrm{min}\). As a result, no further conversion was detected (Fig. S8 in the ESM). The Au content in the liquid reaction mixture was determined by ICP, being very low \((0.02\,\mathrm{ppm})\). Thus, the decrease in activity after five runs could be ascribed to the decreased exposure of active sites manifested by the decrease of the BET surface area (Fig. S7(c) in the ESM), with the agglomeration of Au NPs indicated by TEM images (Fig. S7(d) in the ESM) also contributing to this decrease.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, nanostructured Au/MOF-199 catalysts with ultrafine Au NPs \((<\!2\,\mathrm{nm})\) have been successfully prepared by a rapid one-pot synthesis using microwave irradiation. The loading of Au could be increased up to \(5.13\%\). Moreover, the present one-pot synthesis assisted by microwave irradiation is a general strategy for preparing Ag/MOF-199 and Pt/MOF-199. The Au/MOF-199 catalyst exhibited high activity and excellent selectivity in the \(\mathrm{A}^{3}\)-coupling reaction. Impressively, the TONs and TOFs of Au/MOF-199 with high gold loadings were similar to those obtained for low loadings. The \(5.13\%\mathrm{Au/MOF-199}\) catalyst was shown to possess high potential for practical applications due to its high density of active sites. Furthermore, the above catalyst could be recycled for at least five runs without a significant loss of activity, owing to the confinement effect of MOF-199. Moreover, almost no Au leaching occurred during the reaction. This facile design of ultrafine MNP and MOF nanostructures opens up new opportunities for designing and fabricating high-performance multifunctional catalysts.
